  <h2>What a change order is (and what it is not)</h2>
  <p>
    A construction change order is a written amendment to the contract that changes the scope of
    work, the contract sum, the contract time, or all three. Once signed by both parties it is as
    binding as the original agreement. It is not a request for information (an RFI asks a question
    and changes nothing by itself), and it is not a time-and-materials ticket (a T&amp;M ticket
    records hours and materials for work already directed — it is the backup for a change order, not
    a substitute for one).
  </p>
  <p>
    For a subcontractor the change order does one job above all others: it turns extra work into
    billable contract value <em>before</em> the work is done. Every dollar you put into unsigned extras
    is a dollar you will spend the rest of the project negotiating for.
  </p>

  <h2>The contract-sum ladder — why every change order needs one</h2>
  <p>The most useful part of this form is a five-line table most change order forms leave out:</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Line</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Original contract sum</td><td>Your signed subcontract or accepted proposal</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td>Net change by prior change orders</td><td
          >Sum of every previously approved CO (adds minus deducts)</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td>Contract sum before this change order</td><td>Line 1 + line 2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>This change order</td><td>Net change on this form, after markup</td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td>Revised contract sum</td><td
          >Line 3 + line 4 — the number your next pay application bills against</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Carrying the ladder on every change order means the GC, the owner and your own bookkeeper are
    always looking at the same contract value. When application for payment #7 shows a contract sum
    to date, it should equal the last line of your most recent change order. If it doesn't,
    something was billed twice or never billed at all.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The preview above shows the completed example that ships with the download: a mechanical
    subcontractor on a medical office building adds a condensate drain line after a drawing revision
    (RFI-014 → M-402 Rev. 2). Five priced lines — four adds and one deduct for the PVC drain the
    revision deleted — subtotal to $5,030.00; 15% overhead and profit brings the net change to
    $5,784.50; the ladder carries a $486,200.00 original contract and $5,370.00 of prior change
    orders to a revised $497,354.50; and the contract time grows by three calendar days. That is
    everything a GC's project manager needs to approve it without a phone call.
  </p>

  <h2>Change order vs. RFI vs. T&amp;M ticket</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>RFI</strong> — a question about the documents. Send it the day you spot the gap. If the
      answer adds or removes work, the RFI number goes on the reference line of a change order.
      <a href="/templates/rfi/">RFI template</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>T&amp;M ticket</strong> — the same-day, signed record of hours, materials and equipment
      spent on directed extra work. It proves cost; it does not change the contract. Attach it to the
      change order as backup. <a href="/templates/tm-ticket/">T&amp;M ticket template</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Change order</strong> — the signed agreement on scope, price and time. It is the only one
      of the three that changes what you get paid.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common mistakes this form is built to prevent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Starting on a handshake — the no-work-before-signature line exists for a reason.</li>
    <li>Pricing a lump sum with no lines — a number without backup gets negotiated down.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the time impact — a change with zero days should say zero days, in writing.</li>
    <li>Skipping the ladder — the contract sum drifts, and pay applications stop reconciling.</li>
    <li>
      Losing the reference — a change order that does not cite the RFI or drawing revision is hard
      to defend a year later.
    </li>
    <li>
      Filing it separately from billing — keep the signed CO with the pay application it is billed
      on.
    </li>
  </ul>
